  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... A broad ridge remains in place across the Mid-\nMississippi and Ohio Valley Regions. Hot and unstable conditions \nremain in place with dewpoints in the low 70s and ML CAPE values of \n2500-3000 J/kg. Temperatures of 10C at 700mb can be a good indicator \nof a capped environment and this threshold extends to just north of \nMansfield in the west but south of Youngstown in the east. So far a \nlack of forcing has been a limiting factor for thunderstorms this \nafternoon but that could change this evening. \n\nThe active storm track extends around the periphery of the ridge \nfrom Iowa across Central Lower Michigan to New York. We are seeing \nsome cu trying to build across the snowbelt region with isolated to \nscattered storms possible in this area between 4-8 PM. The first\ncouple showers that have developed have struggled to grow\nvertically. We will have to see if this initial activity has \nstrong enough updrafts to be severe. Later this evening, the \nshortwave crossing lower Michigan will move towards New York and\nexpect the potential for scattered strong to severe storms to \nmove south into Northwest Pennsylvania, likely extending \nwestward into Ohio. Effective shear values will increase to 40+ \nknots this evening coupled with backed flow in the low levels \ndownwind of Lake Erie. The SPC has raised the severe weather \noutlook across New York and extending into Erie County PA to an \nEnhanced Risk (level 3 of 5) while a Slight Risk (level 2 of 5) \nremains from nearly Toledo to Youngstown. Main time frame for \norganized severe storms looks to be in the 7 PM - 11 PM window, \nthen settling south across Northeast Ohio overnight. A few of \nthe CAMs show another push of stronger storms late tonight \nmoving southeast along the boundary but confidence is low in \nthat occurrence. \n\nThe overnight activity will set the stage for Thursday as the ridge \naxis shifts slightly westward with northwest flow aloft. Instability \nwill be very high again and expect to see scattered showers and \nthunderstorms develop with daytime heating during the afternoon. \nThis could initially be focused from northwest to southeast across \nthe eastern counties of Ohio but think we will see an expansion of \nstorms across much of northern Ohio during the evening. Still many \noptions for how this will evolve but the potential for severe \nthunderstorms and heavy rain will continue. PW values of around\n2 inches will support heavy rainfall. Good coverage of \nthunderstorms are likely to persist Thursday night as a stronger\nshortwave trough finally crosses northern Ohio. Showers with \nscattered thunderstorms may linger into Friday but will become \nfocused across Northwest Ohio by Saturday as the overall \nlongwave pattern transitions to northwest flow with a trough \nover New England. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \n\nTemperatures will trend slightly cooler on Thursday as the boundary \nsettles south into the area. The hottest area will be along the I-75 \ncorridor where highs will still be near 90 and heat index values \nwill reach the upper 90s. Given the potential for scattered showers \nand thunderstorms along with partly cloudy skies, a Heat Advisory is \nnot planned for Thursday. This could change overnight if chances of \nprecipitation trend lower in the west or if more sun is expected. \nWill see how the convection plays out first before deciding if \nwe need to an advisory for a few more counties. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3... High pressure will build south out of Canada on \nSaturday and bring cooler and drier conditions through the Holiday \nweekend. High temperatures will return to near normal values in the \nupper 70s to near 80. Dewpoints in the upper 50s to low 60s will \nbring lower humidity. A lower chance of precipitation will continue \nalong the I-75 corridor on Saturday with dry conditions in the \nforecast Sunday through Tuesday.",
